    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity 5yr CAGR 5.2% vs revenue 11.2% / EPS 11.6% (>30% divergence) -- caused by buybacks, not by the business. Owner-FCF CAGRs are negative because FY2020-21 were peak cash years (2020 deferred taxes/comp, 2021 transaction boom) and FY2023 owner FCF was ~0 on a working-capital/bonus unwind; the endpoints, not the trend, drive those figures.",
    "notes": "Owner FCF (reported FCF - SBC - equity-award taxes): FY2020 1,460; FY2021 1,930; FY2022 1,171; FY2023 7; FY2024 1,150; FY2025 1,036. Reported FCF is levered (interest paid 448 in FY2025) -- the forward build is unlevered, so year-1 owner FCF (~1.8bn) sits above the FY2025 levered figure by roughly after-tax interest plus FY2026 growth. SBC-adjusted CAGR sits slightly above reported (SBC has not outgrown cash flow)."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "The resilient ~65% (facilities/BOE, Project Management via Turner & Townsend, Pearce and data-centre technical services) compounds high-single digits while leasing and capital markets complete a normal recovery from the 2023-24 trough. FY2026 lands at the raised core-EPS guide and FY2027 at the >=15% initial guide; thereafter growth fades to ~3.5% as data-centre services decelerate from ~25% toward mid-teens and the transaction cycle matures. Margins expand modestly (6.6% -> 7.3% pre-SBC EBITDA on gross revenue) from infrastructure mix and Advisory operating leverage, capped because the BOE/Project Management revenue carries thin pass-through margins.",
    "bull": "AI data-centre build-out runs at management's ~25%/yr through 2030 and the $10bn infrastructure revenue / $1bn+ EBITDA target is hit; leasing and capital-markets volumes return to 2021-22 levels as rates settle; Investment Management fundraising reopens. Revenue compounds ~10% for five years and pre-SBC EBITDA margin reaches 8.0% as higher-margin Advisory and infrastructure services mix up. Exit at 17x owner FCF, in line with a less-cyclical outsourcing compounder.",
    "bear": "A renewed CRE downturn or higher-for-longer rates hits in 2027-28: capital-markets and leasing fees fall 30-40% as in 2023, Investment Management fundraising stays shut, and an AI-capex pause or power/permitting bottlenecks stall data-centre services. Revenue flat then -4%, pre-SBC margin compresses to 5.3% (Advisory operating deleverage on a people-heavy cost base), with the M&A-funded leverage (corporate debt ~$7.6bn, up from ~$3.3bn at FY2024) limiting buybacks. Recovery to only 6.2% margins and 3% growth; 12x exit reflects a market that re-rates CBRE as a cyclical broker."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "CBRE valued as a cyclical fee-based services operating company on unlevered owner FCF with SBC and equity-award taxes fully expensed and the share count held flat (buybacks fund it). Components are built from GAAP operating income + D&A rather than management's Core EBITDA; acquired-intangible amortization rolls off; acquisitions are not modelled, so growth paths are organic plus already-paid deals. Cyclicality is charged once, through a genuine downturn bear path and a 20% bear weight, not through a WACC premium.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Transaction cycle: leasing and capital-markets fees are the swing factor; a 2023-style drop compresses margin by ~1.3pp",
      "Data-centre services growth (~25%/yr guided) is the main source of above-GDP growth; an AI-capex pause removes it",
      "Acquisition-driven growth: the model does not charge future M&A spend, and does not credit M&A-driven revenue either",
      "Short-term borrowings split (commercial paper/revolver vs warehouse lines) is inferred from XBRL; a ~$0.5bn error moves value ~$1.7/share",
      "NCI valued at ~12x earnings; Turner & Townsend buy-out terms could differ"
    ]
  },
